  "hints": [
    "Only attributes change: <code>%ib = range 2 axis=thread</code>, and <code>axis=upcast</code> on the six 4- and 8-iteration tile loops.",
    "<code>%jb</code> and <code>%k0</code> stay plain loops: <code>%jb</code> is inside the thread loop (no nesting), and <code>%k0</code> has 4 iterations here but would have K-1 in general.",
    "The program already writes C's offset as <code>ib·64 + wi·16 + jb·8 + wj</code>, the slice form, and <code>%acc</code> is only used inside <code>%ib</code>, so it's private. Nothing else needs to change."
  ],
  "explain": "This is the schedule the speed tests use, at a size you can read. The thread loop gives each thread 4 whole rows of C. The upcast loops turn the 4x8 tile into 32 separate accumulators, which the C compiler keeps in registers, and the k loop keeps every sum in order. On a 768x768 GEMM this schedule is 15 to 30 times faster than the same loops without axes, with identical bits."
}
